# {{ project_name }}

Build and operate your startups in your own accounts, using shared knowledge,
automation and tooling from [Deploy Your Startup](https://deploy-your-startup.com).
This Factory starts with no personal startups. Public tooling repositories are
included so you can learn from them and contribute improvements.
Read [VISION.md](VISION.md) and [CONTRIBUTING.md](CONTRIBUTING.md).

## Set up your workspace

The supported operational environment is macOS. Guided
[onboarding](https://deploy-your-startup.com/get-started) creates and sets up this
workspace automatically. To resume setup manually, use Startup CLI v0.1.15 or
newer, install the workspace tools and connect GitHub:

```bash
brew install mise yq gh
# Install uv if it is not already available; see https://docs.astral.sh/uv/
gh auth login -h github.com -p https -s repo,workflow,read:packages,write:packages
gh auth setup-git
startup doctor
mise trust
mise install
mise exec -- ./scripts/init.sh
```

Review `projects.yaml` first. Setup clones the public tooling over HTTPS; it does
not provision servers or deploy an application. Existing project directories are
preserved. If setup reports a clone failure, fix the error and rerun it. Agent
skill installation needs a configured coding agent; follow the printed guidance.
You do not need Kubernetes knowledge to test an idea: bootstrap, deployment and
landing pages work without `kubectl`. Install it (`brew install kubectl`) only
when you want to inspect a full-stack startup's cluster yourself.

Initialize Git if this workspace is not already its own repository, review the
files and commit them, including `.copier-answers.yml`. Use your own repository
for the portfolio; do not publish private project metadata unintentionally.

## Add your first startup

From this workspace root, run:

```bash
startup bootstrap --output-dir projects/startups
```

Review the provider costs and complete the CLI's prompts. The CLI registers the
created Git repository in `projects.yaml`, including when setup needs to resume.
It records deployment status only after separate live verification.
For a repository created outside bootstrap, clone it under `projects/startups`
and run `startup factory register projects/startups/example-app`. The entry uses
its actual remote:

```yaml
projects:
  - name: example-app
    repo: https://github.com/example-owner/example-app.git
```

Record a project even when bootstrap needs to resume. Add `deployed: true`, `url`
and `kubectl_context` only after verifying Actions, DNS, HTTPS and cluster access.
For full-stack deployments, `startup verify --working-directory
projects/startups/example-app` checks the deployment. Follow AGENTS.md for legal
onboarding and security checks before public launch.

Claude Code offers to install the bundled
[dashboard mod](https://github.com/Deploy-your-Startup/startup-factory-mod) when you
trust this folder; `/factory` then shows each startup's website, pods and git state.

Run `mise tasks --all` to discover project commands. Use `scripts/sync.sh` to pull
the hub and registered repositories on another configured machine; it ends with
`mise run registry:check`, which fails when `projects.yaml` and the checkouts
under `projects/` disagree.

## Keep the shared foundation up to date

From a clean, committed workspace, select a published release:

```bash
startup template update --version <release-tag> --dry-run
startup template update --version <release-tag>
```

Review the diff, resolve conflicts, run `mise run test` and commit the update with
`.copier-answers.yml`. Your startup registry and Sprowi settings are preserved.
Review release notes for optional changes to those owner-managed files; new
registry entries and Sprowi defaults are not applied automatically.

Update all registered startups from this workspace:

```bash
./scripts/update-templates.sh          # preview
./scripts/update-templates.sh --apply  # apply locally
```

Each startup's Copier metadata selects its template; the script uses its latest
HEAD. Missing checkouts, repositories without Copier metadata and uncommitted
changes are skipped. Review the diffs, resolve conflicts, run project checks and
commit each update. The script does not pull, commit, push or deploy.

`startup sync` handles shared deployment roles/workflows separately. Pulling this
workspace with Git does not apply upstream Factory template changes.

## Optional agent loop

Run `scripts/init.sh --with-sprowi` only when you want the Sprowi agent loop. It needs
Docker Desktop, sbx and an engine account. See [docs/sprowi.md](docs/sprowi.md) for
setup, budgets, experiments and publishing with your own GitHub App. No App ID,
personal task history or production credentials are bundled.

For daily startup research, ranked ideas and decision memory, run
`scripts/sprowi.sh discover run --engine claude` (or `--engine codex`). Both use
the same sandbox loop and archive results locally without building or deploying.
An optional macOS schedule works without Codex; see
[daily discovery](docs/sprowi.md#daily-discovery-with-claude-or-codex).
